# Environment

Local Debian VM; Claude Code runs in a rootless Podman container (`claude-remote-control-podman`, 1 GB RAM, 1.5 cores). Hardware: 4 cores, 6 GB RAM (about 5.8 usable), NVIDIA GTX 1070 Ti (8 GB VRAM, compute capability 6.1, CUDA 12.4 driver). Outbound traffic goes through a local Squid proxy (`http://127.0.0.1:3128`) behind an AdGuard Home DNS allowlist. claude.ai cloud sandbox settings do not apply.

## Network
- `403` from Squid, or `502` / "Tunnel connection failed" on a host's first request: not on the allowlist. Do not retry, switch mirrors or work around it. Report every blocked host, including secondary download/CDN hosts (for example `files.pythonhosted.org`), so the user can decide.
- `502`, `503`, `429` or resets on a host that already worked this session: upstream throttling or an outage, not a block. Stop, back off, report.
- `407`: check `HTTP_PROXY` / `HTTPS_PROXY` before calling it a block.
- Bulk requests: 1-2 s between calls, descriptive `User-Agent`.
- When the user says they changed the allowlist or asks you to retry, do it.

## Tools
- Language runtimes and light CLIs: `mise` (never global `pip` / `npm` outside a venv or uv environment).
- Heavy native stacks (CAD/mesh, slicers, CFD, CUDA: CadQuery, OpenCascade, manifold3d, OpenLB, Numba, PyTorch): sibling Podman containers. Don't modify `/etc` or system packages in the Claude container.

## Heavy jobs
Run them in sibling containers (`CONTAINER_HOST` is the host user's rootless `podman.socket`; use `--userns=keep-id`, mount `/home/claude/workspace` at the same path). Never use `ulimit -v`.

- Every job gets `--memory` with an equal `--memory-swap`, `--cpuset-cpus`, a time limit, and `--network=none` unless it needs the network. Size them for the job: measure first (`podman stats --no-stream`, `OOMKilled` in `podman inspect`) and set the next limit from the peak. Total across containers stays well inside RAM and 4 cores; at most two CPU solver jobs, on separate core pairs (`0,1` and `2,3`); no build alongside two solver jobs.
- Needs the network: replace `--network=none` with `--network=pasta:-T,3128 -e HTTP_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:3128 -e HTTPS_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:3128 -e NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1,::1`.
- Short jobs (minutes): synchronous `podman run --rm ...`.
- Long jobs: not Bash `run_in_background` or `nohup` (2-hour tool timeout). Start `podman run -d --name <job>` with output to `/home/claude/workspace/<job>.log`, then a `podman wait` trigger that resumes the session:
  ```bash
  ( code=$(podman wait <job>); oom=$(podman inspect <job> --format '{{.State.OOMKilled}}'); podman rm -f <job> >/dev/null 2>&1
    cd /home/claude/workspace && claude --continue -p "Container <job> finished (exit=$code, oom_killed=$oom). Read /home/claude/workspace/<job>.log and report." ) >/dev/null 2>&1 &
  ```
  Check the log shows progress before telling the user the job is healthy.
- Images: `podman pull` / `podman build -t <name> .` (proxied); reuse tagged images.

## GPU
- Only from a sibling container: `--device nvidia.com/gpu=all --security-opt=label=disable`. Quick check: `podman run --rm --device nvidia.com/gpu=all --security-opt=label=disable debian:trixie-slim nvidia-smi`.
- One GPU container at a time. Check free VRAM first and size the problem to fit.
- Pascal: build for `sm_61` (`CUDA_ARCH=61`), CUDA 12.x only (13 dropped Pascal), prefer float.